#
# prima-materia — animal magnetism
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and the question of the edition, then the
# operation with its clock values, then the states it is held to produce, then
# the capacities claimed in those states, then what the operation is said to
# bring about, then the conditions the source sets before any of it, then the
# warnings, then — the part this transmission was opened for — the stopping
# rules, and beside them the three clauses that keep the stopping rules from
# ever being reached. The placements on the consciousness axes stand last.
#
# The tradition instance was registered in traditions/registry-modern.ttl and
# is moved here now that passages have been read.
#
# Why this transmission and not another from the register: SPEC §14 puts the
# testability-driven entrance first, and this is the entrance's clearest case
# in the whole register. The work read states a trial of five days, a rule of
# three or four sittings, a term of nearly a month, a three-outcome decision
# test inside a single sitting, and a rate of three quarters of patients. Very
# little else in this holding states a number at which its own claim would be
# due. What the reading found is that the same work supplies, in the same
# chapters, the clauses that make each of those numbers unreachable — and that
# finding is worth more than the numbers were.
#

pmt:Mesmerism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mesmerism"@en, "Mesmerismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of an animal magnetism held to pass between bodies and to be directed by the will of an operator through passes of the hand, together with the induced states its practitioners described: the crisis, the magnetic sleep, and the somnambulism in which the subject is held to perceive the seat of his own disease."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording: Deleuze's manual of 1825 in Hartshorn's English of 1843, chapters I, II, III, V, VI and VIII, that is the principles, the processes, the effects and their indications, somnambulism, the choice of an operator, and the dangers. Left unread in that volume: chapter IV on accessory means, chapter VII on the application to particular diseases, chapter IX on developing the faculty in oneself, chapter X on the studies, and the whole of the appendices."@en ;
    skos:note "The edition question has to be settled before any citation from this work can be read, and it is the reason every source line below carries a chapter and a page. Hartshorn's revised edition of 1843 prints Deleuze to page 216 and then an Appendix and a New Appendix of the translator's own notes and of letters from American correspondents, which are not Deleuze, are forty years later, and are editorialised by Hartshorn inside their own pages. Nothing below is drawn from beyond page 216."@en ;
    skos:note "The translation is named in each source line for the same reason. Deleuze wrote in French in 1825; what was read is Hartshorn's English. Where the wording of a stopping rule decides a node — the five days, the three or four sittings, the nearly a month — a reader who wants to press it should set the French first edition beside this, and that collation has not been made here."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stands as documented and this is not a formality. It runs in both directions and both are stated inside the corpus. Backwards: the operation is the laying on of hands with a theory attached, the 1784 commission's own historical introduction traces the doctrine to physicians of the preceding age, and Deleuze names Mesmer and Puységur as his predecessors and takes the whole of his doctrine from the latter. Forwards is the heavier one: this is the transmission out of which the nineteenth-century hypnotism literature and, through it, a great part of what now circulates as the ancient doctrine of older traditions came. A parallel between this tradition and a later one is reception until shown otherwise."@en ;
    skos:note "What this tradition is good for in this holding, and it is unusual: the operator is not the subject. Almost everything else here has a practitioner who does something to himself and reports the result. Here one person acts and another reports, which makes the reports checkable in a way the rest are not — and makes the presence of a third person a question the transmission has to answer. It answers it, at length, and the answer is the material of generalizations/the-unsympathetic-witness.ttl."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Mesmer, Mémoire sur la découverte du magnétisme animal; Puységur, Mémoires pour servir à l'histoire du magnétisme animal; Deleuze, Instruction pratique sur le magnétisme animal" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, pp. 21-27" .

#
# The operation, with its clock values
#

pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Magnetizing by the long pass"@en, "Magnetisieren mit grossen Strömen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operator seats the patient, places himself facing him with the patient's knees between his own, and takes the patient's thumbs between his fingers so that the inner surfaces touch, remaining so from two to five minutes or until he perceives an equal degree of heat between his thumbs and the patient's. He then raises his hands to the head, lays them on both shoulders for about a minute, draws them down the arms to the fingertips, repeats that pass five or six times, holds them a moment on the head, brings them down before the face at a distance of one or two inches to the pit of the stomach, lets them remain there about two minutes, and descends slowly to the knees or the feet. A pass from head to feet takes about half a minute. Passes are magnetic descending and not ascending, which is why the hands are turned obliquely when raised. The sitting is closed by passes prolonged beyond the hands and feet with the fingers shaken at each, transverse passes before the face and breast at three or four inches, and, with the patient standing, seven or eight passes from above the head to the floor with one hand before the body and one behind."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at this length because the length is the point. This is the most exactly specified operation in the holding: every element carries either a count or a duration in minutes, the direction of each movement is prescribed, and the reason for the direction is stated. A transmission that specifies to this degree can be performed by a stranger from the page, which is the condition under which any of its claims could be put to a trial at all."@en ;
    skos:note "The source states in the same chapter that the choice of process is not itself essential, that other operators follow their own, and that the results are nearly the same; and at chapter I it says the will suffices to give the fluid its direction. The exactness above therefore does not belong to the doctrine — it is offered as a method to adopt so as not to be embarrassed. That tension is the transmission's own and is carried here rather than resolved: the same book that prescribes half a minute for a pass says that the passes could be otherwise."@en ;
    skos:note "The session length and its frequency stand with it: the first sittings about an hour, and once the communication is established half an hour or three quarters, the sittings periodical and equal in duration, at the same hour every day, and above all the agreed hour not changed for many days in succession. Where the operator can give only two or three sittings a week the source has magnetized water supply the direct action."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. II, pp. 28-33, and chap. III, p. 46" .

#
# The states
#

pmc:MagnetismeRapport a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rapport, the established communication"@en, "Rapport, die hergestellte Verbindung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The induced condition in which the operator exerts an influence upon the patient, there being between them a communication of the vital principle. It is established by taking the thumbs, and strengthened by setting the ten fingers against the patient's so that the balls touch. Once well established it is renewed in each succeeding sitting at the instant of beginning, so that touching is no longer needed; it is sometimes established at once and sometimes only after a long trial; and it is rare not to have it established at the first sitting."@en ;
    skos:note "The pivot of the whole transmission, and the place where its testability is decided. Rapport is what makes the action selective — the source has the patient understand only those put in communication with him, and later makes the breaking of the communication by a distracted spectator the cause of shocks. Every claim in this file that could be checked from outside runs through it, and so does every excuse for a check that fails."@en ;
    skos:note "The source states that experienced operators generally perceive in themselves when it takes place. That locates the criterion in the operator and nowhere else, and it is the reason the three-outcome test below matters: it is the one place where the book offers a sign of the state that somebody other than the operator could read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 8, p. 22, and chap. II, pp. 31-32" .

pmc:MagnetismeCrise a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The crisis"@en, "Die Krise"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any state differing from the natural one and resulting from the magnetic influence, manifested by sharp pains in a part of the body, a sense of suffocation, nervous movement, spasms, considerable perspiration, the impossibility of opening the eyes, drowsiness, sleep or somnambulism. The operator is to let it develop, calm the spasm by degrees, concentrate the action on the seat of the pain and then draw it off, and on no account leave the patient or let anyone disturb him until the state has entirely ceased."@en ;
    skos:note "The source reports and then qualifies Mesmer's rule that there are no cures without crises: read as meaning that recovery requires a change of state it is trivially true, read as meaning that the cure is worked by a sudden change with evident symptoms it is not always true, since many diseases are cured by slow amelioration in which no moment can be marked. That is a transmission correcting its own founder on the point where his claim was strongest, and it is entered as the correction it is."@en ;
    skos:note "The word is used here in a narrower sense than physicians use it, and the source says so: operators came to call somnambulism the crisis and somnambulists crisiacs. A reader comparing this with the medical literature of the period is comparing two senses of one word."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 47-49" .

pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magnetic somnambulism"@en, "Magnetischer Somnambulismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "A mode of existence in which the person appears asleep, answers his operator without waking, can execute movements, and on returning to the natural state retains no remembrance of what passed. The eyes are closed; he generally understands only those put in communication with him; the external organs of sense are all or nearly all asleep, and yet he experiences sensations by another means, an internal sense being roused in him which is perhaps the centre of the others. He is subject to the influence of his operator, and that influence may be useful or injurious according to the operator's disposition and conduct."@en ;
    skos:note "It is stated to occur only in a small number of cases, and the source devotes a chapter to it precisely because it changes the manner of operating. That rarity is worth holding beside the fact that it is the state on which every capacity in this file depends."@en ;
    skos:note "The dependence on the operator is given a stated limit in the same work: the somnambulist preserves his reason and the use of his will, cannot be made to reveal a secret he has reason to conceal or to do things contrary to the principles he holds awake, and a reprehensible act of volition would make him revolt and throw him into convulsions. The transmission thus asserts obedience and its boundary in the same breath, and the placement on the agency axis below says so rather than picking one half."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 68-69, and chap. VIII, pp. 165-166" .

pmc:MagnetismeDeplacementDeLaDouleur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The displacement of the pain"@en, "Die Verlagerung des Schmerzes"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the most ordinary effects: the operation dislodges the pain and makes it pass down in the direction of the current given to the fluid. Where the dislodged pain does not at first reach the extremities it is to be forced thither in subsequent sittings; where gout seated in the head descends and is arrested at the breast or stomach the action is to be continued until it is conducted to the feet. The displacement is always a proof of the efficacy of the operation."@en ;
    skos:note "The last sentence is the one to hold. A movement of the reported site of a pain is taken as establishing that the agent acted, and the same chapter has the operator prescribe where the pain is to go next — so the sign is produced by the same hand that reads it. This is the cheapest of the three unfalsifiability clauses in the file and the only one the source states as a rule rather than as a qualification."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also, in a narrow way, the one claim here a bystander could check in a single sitting: whether a patient who located a pain in the head locates it in the knees twenty minutes later is a report anybody in the room can take down. The examination beside this file does not use it, because the 1784 commission did not test it; the address is recorded here for whoever does."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 44-45" .

#
# The capacities
#

pmc:MagnetismeLucidite a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lucidity, the clairvoyance of the somnambulist"@en, "Lucidité, die Hellsicht des Somnambulen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A faculty developed in some somnambulists: seeing without the aid of the eyes, hearing without the ears, seeing at a distance, reading the thoughts, appreciating time with rigorous exactitude, and having a presage of the future. It requires the somnambulist to concentrate his faculties upon a single object without distraction and without any foreign influence changing the direction of his mind. It is not always accompanied by a perfect clairvoyance, is often relative to a certain order of ideas, and is variable in its intensity from one moment to another."@en ;
    skos:note "The source grants the faculty and then removes, one by one, every condition under which it could be demonstrated: it is rare, it is not extended equally to all objects, it varies between moments, it is destroyed by an unsympathetic witness, it is destroyed by the presence of a physician not familiar with the practice, it declines in somnambulists consulted by several operators, and it is not to be put to any proof. What remains is a capacity asserted together with the grounds for dismissing every failed demonstration of it."@en ;
    skos:note "The source's own judgement of the professional consultants it examined is the most useful sentence in the chapter and is entered as his: among those he observed there is not one he has not known to commit errors, and not one that has not shown him proofs of clairvoyance. He also reports having been for three days the dupe of a feigned somnambulism."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 84-85, and chap. VIII, pp. 177-178 and 185" .

pmc:MagnetismeAutoDiagnostic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The somnambulist perceives the seat of his own disease"@en, "Der Somnambule erkennt den Sitz seiner eigenen Krankheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity on which the practice turns: the somnambulist perceives the seat of his own disorder, sees the lesion in one part, states the nature, cause and consequences of his complaint, names the crises that will bring on the cure, prescribes the remedial means and the hour at which he is to be operated upon, and describes the effects his prescriptions will produce."@en ;
    skos:note "The boundary of the capacity is stated and it is a real one: it is rare that he sees the situation, form and tissue of his organs, especially of those not affected, so anatomical questions yield vague and perhaps erroneous views. The source draws the line exactly where it is useful — he will not mistake in foretelling a crisis, pointing out a remedy or describing its effects, but could easily give explanations that would be ridiculous in proportion to the interest shown in hearing them."@en ;
    skos:note "And the failure mode is named with it: where several diseases are present the somnambulist fixes on the one that gives him most uneasiness and may prescribe for it something injurious to another. One worked case is given, of a patient with affected lungs and an impaired stomach who was talked out of her own prescription and thanked the operator a fortnight later. So the transmission holds, of the faculty it rests on, that its bearer is to be overruled."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 74, 81 and 84-85" .

pmc:MagnetismePrevision a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The faculty of prevision and its stated limit"@en, "Das Vermögen der Vorschau und seine ausgesprochene Grenze"@de ;
    skos:definition "The most inconceivable of the faculties, said never to extend beyond a certain number of objects and to be conditional; and of which the source states that if there are facts enough to prove its existence, there is not a case where the exactitude of its application can be reckoned upon."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence is the sharpest self-limitation in the file and it is worth more than the claim it limits. A faculty whose existence is asserted and whose application is declared never reckonable cannot be brought to any trial, and the source says so without appearing to notice that it has said so. Braid would make the same point against the tradition thirty years later as an objection; here it stands inside the manual as a counsel of prudence."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter forbids consulting somnambulists on political affairs, on the dogmas of religion and on metaphysics, and reports men otherwise well informed becoming the dupes of their visions and predictions. The prohibition and the limit are one thought."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 185-188" .

pmc:MagnetismePerceptionParLOperateur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The operator who perceives the seat of the disease"@en, "Der Magnetiseur, der den Sitz der Krankheit wahrnimmt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A capacity claimed for the operator and not the subject: among those at Paris who devote themselves to the practice the source says he knows some who perceive the seat of the disease and modify their action accordingly, and some who pass into a sort of demi-somnambulism during which they operate with much discernment."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as its own node because it is the only capacity in the file whose bearer is the person making the report, and because it removes the one structural advantage the tradition had. Everywhere else the claim is checkable in principle: one person acts, another reports, and the reports can be collected. Here the operator both acts and reports, and the transmission has arrived at the shape of every other tradition in this holding."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VI, pp. 113-114" .

pmc:MagnetismeConsultationPourAbsent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Consultation for an absent person by a worn object"@en, "Beratung für einen Abwesenden über einen getragenen Gegenstand"@de ;
    skos:definition "Some somnambulists consult for persons absent and unknown to them: hair of the patient, or something worn for several days on the stomach next to the skin, suffices to put them in close communication with him, whereupon they describe his physical and moral condition exactly and minutely. The object is to be enveloped in paper and the packet not opened when presented."@en ;
    skos:note "The one claim in this file that is cleanly testable by a procedure the transmission itself prescribes, and the procedure is already half a blind: the packet is closed, the operator does not open it, and a second packet from a person with a different complaint would complete the design. The source does not take that step and nothing in the work suggests it occurred to him."@en ;
    skos:note "Before introducing a patient to a somnambulist the operator is to let him touch something the patient has worn, so that he may say whether he feels repugnance to it or sees danger in being put in communication. The same object thus serves as the channel of the diagnosis and as the ground for refusing to make it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, p. 82, and chap. VIII, p. 178" .

#
# What the operation is said to bring about
#

pmc:GrandsCouransYieldRelief a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Magnetizing by the long pass brings relief and cure"@en, "Das Magnetisieren mit grossen Strömen bringt Erleichterung und Heilung hervor"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim the practice exists for: the operation develops what physicians call the forces medicatrices, seconds the efforts nature makes to relieve itself, and thereby soothes and cures. It is said to cure much more promptly and better in country people and children than in those who have lived in the world and taken much medicine."@en ;
    skos:note "The strength of the claim is stated in one number and it is the only rate in the file: at least three fourths of the patients feel the effects. That is carried on its own node because it is the one place where the transmission makes itself due."@en ;
    skos:note "Four worked cases stand behind it in the chapters read, all of them the source's own and none of them controlled: a paralysed old soldier whose wife made the passes, a caretaker's wife cured in fifteen days, a lady whose catarrh went at the first sitting and left three days of pain in the limbs, and a blind girl restored from eleven months of head pain in five sittings. The first is cited by the source to a published medical account of the same patient, which is the nearest thing to an outside witness anywhere in the work."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MagnetismeDeplacementDeLaDouleur ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, §§ 21, 27-28, pp. 24-26, and chap. III, pp. 53-54" .

pmc:GrandsCouransYieldSomnambulisme a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Magnetizing brings on somnambulism, and is not to be used for it"@en, "Das Magnetisieren bringt Somnambulismus hervor und soll nicht dazu benutzt werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation is held to produce the somnambulic state where nature is disposed to that crisis: the fluid is then carried of itself to the brain, and the tendency shows in tranquillity, closed eyes and sleep. The source states that he has produced somnambulism by operating from the foot of the bed, without establishing the communication by touching."@en ;
    skos:note "A yielding claim whose own source forbids aiming at it. Operators who charge the head obtain a forced slumber, a reflux of blood towards the brain and partial crises which are of no utility, and the method is not without danger. The claim and the prohibition are in the same paragraph."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. II, p. 33, and chap. V, pp. 70-71" .

pmc:SomnambulismeYieldsAutoDiagnostic a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Somnambulism brings the perception of one's own disease"@en, "Der Somnambulismus bringt die Wahrnehmung der eigenen Krankheit hervor"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the somnambulic state the patient is held to perceive the seat of his own disorder and to be able to state its cause, name the crises that will bring the cure, and prescribe the means."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the yielding on which the practice's medical claim rests, and the source attaches to it the conditions that make it unexaminable: the somnambulist must be occupied with nothing but himself, must not be asked a question of curiosity, must not be shown to a physician unfamiliar with the practice, and must not be put to any proof. The capacity is asserted and its demonstration is prohibited."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MagnetismeAutoDiagnostic ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 74-75 and 80-82" .

#
# The conditions set before any of it
#

pmc:MagnetiserPresupposesWillConfidenceBenevolence a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The operation presupposes will, confidence and benevolence together"@en, "Die Operation setzt Wille, Zutrauen und Wohlwollen zusammen voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first condition of action is to exercise the will; the second is the confidence the operator has in his own powers; the third is benevolence, the desire of doing good. One of these may supply the others to a certain point, but for the action to be at once energetic and salutary the three must be united."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in the same numbered series: if the desire of doing good be not united to the will to act, there will be some effects, but these effects will be irregular. That is a consequence, and it is also the escape route — irregular effects are what an unsuccessful trial produces anyway, so the condition can absorb every failure without ever being violated in a way a reader could point to."@en ;
    skos:note "The same series makes confidence unnecessary in the patient: one can act equally on those who believe and those who do not, it sufficing that the patient yields himself passively without resistance. That asymmetry is the transmission's own answer to the charge of imagination and it is the claim the 1784 commission's blindfold design was built against."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeRapport ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If the desire of doing good be not united to the will to act, there will be some effects, but these effects will be irregular."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, §§ 4, 9, 14, 26, pp. 21-26" .

pmc:MagnetiserPresupposesTheOperatorsHealth a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The operation presupposes the operator's own health"@en, "Die Operation setzt die Gesundheit des Magnetiseurs voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because the fluid emanating from the operator exercises a physical influence on the patient, the operator must be in good health; and because the influence acts in time on the patient's moral condition, he must also be worthy of esteem for the uprightness of his mind and the honesty of his character."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity on a consequence the source states twice and illustrates with a case: rheumatic pains, nervous affections and especially organic diseases are communicated from the operator to the patient in proportion to the thoroughness of the communication, and a person whose lungs are delicate cannot without danger operate on one whose lungs are affected. The case is of an operator with an inflammation of the larynx who sent a magnetized handkerchief each evening; the patient contracted the same disease and was in the greatest danger when he died."@en ;
    skos:note "The source also reports, of one operator, that she commonly takes the disease of the person she operates on — having the sensation and symptoms of it for several days — and instances gout and an ophthalmia, which he notes are not contagious. He says he cannot account for it. A claim of that shape is checkable by ordinary means and the transmission does not check it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeRapport ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Rheumatic pains, nervous affections, and especially organic diseases, are communicated from the magnetizer to the person magnetized, with facility proportioned to the thoroughness of the communication between them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 15, p. 23, and chap. VIII, pp. 171-172" .

pmc:TreatmentPresupposesItsOwnContinuation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The treatment presupposes that it will be continued"@en, "Die Behandlung setzt ihre eigene Fortsetzung voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "A real danger of the practice is the interruption of a treatment begun and the failure to sustain a crisis that has been excited and that nature cannot develop and terminate unaided. It is nothing in slight and recent indispositions and very serious in organic and long-standing diseases; one ought not to attempt the action at all except when sure of continuing it as long as necessary."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity and it is the best-supported necessity in the file, because the source gives two dated cases in which the consequence followed and in the first of them names himself as the cause. A lady relieved of a monthly headache in half an hour was two days later seized with a fever that lasted six weeks; he does not doubt that it came of the humour he had displaced and would not have occurred had he continued for a month. A blind girl operated upon once with energy for an hour by a student who then broke off suffered eleven months of head pain."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one claim in the file whose falsifying condition is ordinary and within reach, and it runs against the tradition's interest: it predicts that interrupting a course of these operations is followed by an identifiable harm at a rate above that of never beginning one. No protocol is written on it here. Its address is this node."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeCrise ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A person might do much mischief by magnetizing with energy only once to drive off an internal pain caused by a tumor, or by a humor which, for many years, attacks an organ at certain periods; and the accidents which have occurred by the rude interruption of a treatment ought not to be attributed to magnetism, but to the imprudence of the magnetizer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 168-170" .

pmc:LuciditePresupposesUndividedAttention a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Lucidity presupposes an undivided attention"@en, "Die Lucidité setzt ungeteilte Aufmerksamkeit voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "For the proper exercise of clairvoyance the somnambulist must concentrate his faculties upon a single object, without distraction, without trouble, and without the intervention of any foreign influence to change the direction of his mind; the more his attention is distracted by various subjects, the less of it he gives to the essential object."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity on a consequence the source gives in plain terms: made to talk beyond the seat of his own disorder he will furnish only vague and perhaps erroneous views, and questions of curiosity excite his vanity, after which nothing he says can be depended upon. The greater part of somnambulists, even in good hands, are said to have lost a portion of their faculties because many persons in succession were allowed to see them."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the prerequisite from which the whole apparatus of exclusions below follows, and it is the load-bearing member of the generalization formed from this file: the condition of the capacity is stated in such a way that any observation of it destroys it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:MagnetismeLucidite ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MagnetismeRapport ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If you make him talk beyond this, you will obtain from him only vague and perhaps erroneous views; and when you have once awakened in him this sentiment of vanity, to which somnambulists are in general very much inclined, you can no more depend upon any thing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 75-76 and 84" .
